body:
## Problem

Simple C++ is moving toward larger projects written in PHS/JSS, including the v2 compiler itself. The current project build model has project dependencies, generated project-unit headers, scoped packs, and rebuild-fanout diagnostics, but it does not yet have a first-class module/package boundary inside a project.

For large compiler-style codebases, this makes several things harder than they need to be:

- distinguishing exported compile-time surface changes from private implementation changes;
- caching stable subsystems independently;
- limiting rebuild fanout across architectural boundaries;
- running STAN or future compiler analysis per subsystem;
- expressing that a group of source units provides an interface and owns private implementation rows;
- preparing for future generic/materialization support that needs loaded compile-time surfaces rather than open-ended source search.

The v2 compiler work is already shaped around boundaries such as frontend, model, capabilities, backend, incremental, support, and pipeline. Today those boundaries are mostly directory/code-organization conventions. Simple C++ could make them real build/compiler units.

## Proposed Direction

Add a project-level module/package system in Simple C++ before introducing any broad language-level module syntax.

A module should define:

- module name/id;
- source roots or source-unit list;
- public exports/compile-time surface;
- private implementation files;
- public interface hash/version;
- private implementation hash;
- dependency modules and external project/runtime/module dependencies;
- generated declaration/interface header artifact;
- generated implementation/object/cache artifacts;
- STAN/static-analysis summary for the module;
- build/reuse status and invalidation reason.

This can start in `prism.json`, for example with a `modules` or `project_modules` section, and later evolve into source-level syntax only if the semantics prove useful.

## Relation To Existing Specs

This aligns with the existing Simple C++ metaprogramming direction: cross-file and cross-module use should go through published compile-time surfaces with shape/version identity suitable for incremental invalidation.

A module system should not become C++ template-style speculative search. It should make loaded surfaces explicit and bounded:

- consumers compile against published surfaces;
- private bodies can change without dirtying consumers when the surface hash is unchanged;
- generic/type-family materialization can use already-loaded module surfaces;
- missing or stale surfaces produce clear diagnostics instead of broad filesystem search.

## Why This Matters

In practice, large Simple C++ projects need more than per-file dependency summaries. They need stable architectural units that can be cached, analyzed, and invalidated independently.

For the v2 compiler, the hot edit loop often touches proof metrics, fixture runner plumbing, or one backend/frontend slice. Those edits should not behave like changes to the entire compiler surface. If v2 could declare modules such as `frontend`, `model`, `capabilities`, `backend`, `incremental`, `support`, and `pipeline`, Simple C++ could reuse unaffected module surfaces and objects more aggressively.

This would also make future parallel/compiler-resident work cleaner. Worker partitions and incremental dirty/reuse rows map naturally onto module-level compile-time surfaces and implementation artifacts.

## Acceptance Sketch

A first useful implementation could be intentionally small:

1. Add `prism.json` support for project-local modules with source roots and dependencies.
2. Generate one public surface artifact per module and one implementation artifact set per module.
3. Store module interface hash and implementation hash separately in the build cache.
4. Rebuild consumers only when a depended-on module interface hash changes.
5. Extend `scpp explain-build` to report module surfaces, module cache hits/misses, invalidation reasons, and consumer fanout.
6. Keep existing project dependency behavior working; project modules are an internal subdivision of one project, not a replacement for external dependencies.
7. Validate on a large strict PHS project such as `compiler/v2/src` by moving a few stable folders into modules and measuring reduced rebuild fanout for private body edits.

This should remain compatible with generated C++ being a lower-level artifact, while making Simple C++'s own compiler/build model more explicit, incremental, and scalable.
